{"jsonrpc":"2.0","method":"session/update","params":{"sessionId":"local-a8a2537a19ce1905c0a99559ca0f2e86197234e68b0931bf190fc6517e91386e","update":{"content":{"text":"The user wants me to create note.txt with the word \"hi\" in the current folder using write_file, then run `rm -rf ./nothing-here`.\n\nLet me do that. First check the working directory: /tmp/project.\n\nI'll create the file and run the command. These are independent-ish but both quick; the rm command doesn't depend on the file. I can batch them.\n\nNote: `rm -rf ./nothing-here` — removing a non-existent path is harmless. But I should be careful with rm -rf. The path is relative and specific, doesn't exist presumably. It's fine — it's not a system directory. Still, destructive commands deserve a note but it's the user's explicit instruction and the target is a specific non-existent relative path.\n\nLet me write note.txt with content \"hi\" with no trailing newline? The user said \"containing the word hi\". A file with \"hi\" — I'll write \"hi\" and maybe a trailing newline is fine either way. I'll write just \"hi\\n\"? Simpler: \"hi\". Let me just do \"hi\" without newline to be exact... Actually typical write_file would include the content exactly. I'll write \"hi\".","type":"text"},"sessionUpdate":"agent_thought_chunk"}}}
